          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of King Hussein</strong><br>\nKing Hussein was born on November 14, 1935, in Amman, Jordan. He was the eldest child of King Talal and Queen Zein Al-Sharaf Talal. Hussein began his education in Amman, but in his teenage years, he attended Victoria College in Alexandria, Egypt, and later the Harrow School in England. He also spent a brief period at the Royal Military Academy Sandhurst, which is where British Army officers receive their training.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ascension to the Throne</strong><br>\nIn 1952, Hussein became the King of Jordan after his father, King Talal, was deemed unfit to rule due to illness. He was crowned on May 2, 1953, when he turned 18, as is customary for a young monarch. King Hussein's early reign was marked by instability as he navigated the complexities of Middle Eastern politics and the rising issue of Palestinian refugees in Jordan.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Domestic Policies</strong><br>\nKing Hussein focused on building Jordan's economy and infrastructure. He implemented plans to modernize agriculture and industry, which helped improve the standard of living. Hussein was known for his efforts to maintain Jordanian national unity, often promoting policies that encouraged the integration of various ethnic and religious groups within the country.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
