          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Foreign Relations</strong><br>\nThroughout his reign, King Hussein worked to position Jordan as a significant player in Middle Eastern affairs. He maintained a neutral stance in the Cold War, receiving aid from both the United States and the Soviet Union. His foreign policy was characterized by a series of alliances and realignments, reflecting the changing dynamics of the region.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The 1967 and 1973 Wars</strong><br>\nKing Hussein's leadership was critical during the Six-Day War in 1967 and the Yom Kippur War in 1973. Jordan lost the West Bank to Israel in 1967, which was a significant blow to the country. Despite the losses, Hussein's ability to keep Jordan stable and sovereign was seen as a testament to his leadership skills.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Peace with Israel</strong><br>\nIn 1994, King Hussein signed a peace treaty with Israel, ending the state of war between the two countries. The treaty was a historic step towards peace in the Middle East, though it was met with mixed reactions from the Jordanian people and the Arab world.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
